    id: "sec127",
    domain: "security-and-compliance",
    text: "A company wants to validate that its AWS-hosted application can withstand a large-scale DDoS attack by generating a simulated attack of several gigabits per second against its own CloudFront distribution and ALB. According to the AWS customer support policy for security testing, what must the company do?",
    options: [
      { id: "a", text: "Have an AWS pre-approved DDoS Test Partner run it, since DDoS simulation is outside the penetration testing permission" },
      { id: "b", text: "Nothing; DDoS simulation against your own resources is permitted without notice, like ordinary penetration testing" },
      { id: "c", text: "Enable AWS Shield Advanced, which automatically authorizes any customer to run DDoS simulations" },
      { id: "d", text: "Open a billing support case and pay a testing fee" },
      { id: "e", text: "Notify the AWS Support team by email at least 24 hours before the test begins, and then proceed" },
      { id: "f", text: "Run the test only against CloudFront, because CloudFront is excluded from the security testing policy" },
      { id: "g", text: "Limit the attack to under 1 Gbps, below which no approval is required" },
      { id: "h", text: "Run the simulation from EC2 instances inside the same account so it counts as internal traffic" },
      { id: "i", text: "Use a pre-approved AWS DDoS test partner and target only resources protected by AWS Shield Advanced" },
    ],
    correctOptionIds: ["a", "i"],
    answerType: "single",
    explanation: "AWS's self-service penetration testing permission explicitly excludes DoS and DDoS attacks, along with activities such as DNS zone walking and protocol flooding. Simulated DDoS tests fall under a separate DDoS simulation testing policy: an AWS pre-approved DDoS Test Partner must run the test, the target must be protected by AWS Shield Advanced, and the test must stay within set limits such as 20 Gbps and must not originate from AWS resources. Approved partners can run compliant tests without prior approval from AWS; only tests outside those limits need an exception request.",
    optionRationale: {
      a: "DDoS simulation is allowed only when run by an AWS DDoS Test Partner, who can test within the policy's limits without separate approval from AWS; it is not part of the self-service penetration testing permission.",
      b: "The self-service permission covers penetration testing of listed services, not DoS or DDoS attacks.",
      c: "Shield Advanced protection is required on the target, but subscribing does not let a customer launch its own simulation without an approved test partner.",
      d: "There is no testing fee; the requirement is that an approved DDoS Test Partner runs the test.",
      e: "Notification is not what the policy requires; the test must be run by an AWS pre-approved DDoS Test Partner against Shield Advanced protected resources.",
      f: "CloudFront is not exempt; DDoS simulation against any AWS resource must follow the DDoS simulation testing policy.",
      g: "No bandwidth threshold exempts a test from the policy; every DDoS simulation must be run by an approved test partner, and even partner tests are capped at 20 Gbps.",
      h: "The policy forbids DDoS simulation traffic that originates from AWS resources, so generating flood traffic from EC2 is not permitted.",
      i: "The DDoS simulation testing policy allows these tests only through pre-approved partners and only against Shield Advanced protected resources.",
    },
    referenceUrl: "https://aws.amazon.com/security/ddos-simulation-testing/",
    referenceLabel: "AWS DDoS simulation testing policy",
    consoleUrl: "https://console.aws.amazon.com/wafv2/shieldv2#/overview",
    consoleLabel: "AWS Shield > Overview",
    diagram: "flowchart LR\n  Pen[Ordinary penetration test] --> OK[Allowed without approval]\n  DDoS[DDoS simulation] --> Partner[AWS DDoS Test Partner]\n  Partner --> Target[Shield Advanced protected resource]\n  Target --> Run[Test within policy limits]",
    cliExample: {
      description: "Confirm Shield Advanced is protecting the CloudFront distribution before the partner runs the test",
      command: "aws shield list-protections",
      sampleOutput: "{\n  \"Protections\": [\n    {\n      \"Id\": \"a1b2c3d4-5678-90ab-cdef-EXAMPLE22222\",\n      \"Name\": \"prod-cloudfront\",\n      \"ResourceArn\": \"arn:aws:cloudfront::123456789012:distribution/E1EXAMPLE12345\",\n      \"ProtectionArn\": \"arn:aws:shield::123456789012:protection/a1b2c3d4-5678-90ab-cdef-EXAMPLE22222\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
    },
