    id: "sec177",
    domain: "security-and-compliance",
    text: "A company with 60 accounts in AWS Organizations wants to guarantee that no account can make an Amazon Machine Image (AMI) public and that every new EC2 instance requires IMDSv2, without writing and maintaining SCP statements for each EC2 API. Which AWS Organizations feature enforces such service-level baseline configuration across all accounts?",
    options: [
      { id: "a", text: "Declarative policies" },
      { id: "b", text: "Tag policies" },
      { id: "c", text: "Backup policies" },
      { id: "d", text: "AI services opt-out policies" },
      { id: "e", text: "Service control policies (SCPs) with a single wildcard statement" },
      { id: "f", text: "Resource control policies (RCPs)" },
      { id: "g", text: "AWS Config conformance packs" },
      { id: "h", text: "Chatbot policies" },
      { id: "i", text: "Declarative policies that set durable EC2 settings" },
    ],
    correctOptionIds: ["a", "i"],
    answerType: "single",
    explanation: "Declarative policies are an AWS Organizations policy type that lets you declare and enforce the desired baseline configuration of an AWS service across your organization, such as blocking public access for AMIs and EBS snapshots, requiring IMDSv2, or disabling the EC2 serial console. The service itself enforces the setting, so it cannot be overridden by IAM principals in member accounts, and you do not have to model every relevant API call in an SCP.",
    optionRationale: {
      a: "Correct. Declarative policies express service configuration intent and the service enforces it organization-wide.",
      b: "Tag policies standardize tag keys and values; they do not control AMI visibility or IMDS settings.",
      c: "Backup policies centrally manage AWS Backup plans.",
      d: "AI services opt-out policies control whether AWS AI services may use your content for improvement.",
      e: "SCPs restrict IAM actions and would still require modeling each relevant EC2 API; the question asks for a feature that avoids that.",
      f: "RCPs set maximum permissions on resources such as S3 buckets and KMS keys; they do not configure EC2 service-level settings like IMDSv2 or AMI public access.",
      g: "AWS Config conformance packs detect non-compliant resources after the fact; they do not enforce a service baseline.",
      h: "Chatbot policies control which chat clients can be used in the organization; they are unrelated to EC2 settings.",
      i: "Declarative policies state the desired EC2 configuration once, and EC2 itself keeps enforcing it in every account.",
    },
    referenceUrl: "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/organizations/latest/userguide/orgs_manage_policies_declarative_policies.html",
    referenceLabel: "Declarative policies - AWS Organizations",
