    correctOptionIds: ["a", "i"],
    answerType: "single",
    explanation: "Application Load Balancers can authenticate users natively. An 'authenticate-cognito' or 'authenticate-oidc' action on a listener rule redirects unauthenticated users to the identity provider, validates the returned token, and only then forwards the request to the target, adding user claims as HTTP headers. This offloads sign-in from the application entirely and requires no code changes.",
    optionRationale: {
      a: "Correct. ALB built-in authentication integrates with Cognito user pools or any OIDC-compliant IdP.",
      b: "Shield Advanced protects against DDoS; it does not authenticate users.",
      c: "Rewriting the application contradicts the requirement to leave the code unchanged.",
      d: "Network ACLs filter by IP address and port; they have no concept of user identity.",
      e: "AWS WAF filters requests by patterns but cannot perform an interactive sign-in with a corporate identity provider.",
      f: "A VPN restricts network reachability; it does not authenticate individual users to the application.",
      g: "IAM Identity Center permission sets grant access to AWS accounts and applications, not to arbitrary HTTP requests through an ALB.",
      h: "ALB target groups have no IAM authorization setting; authentication is configured on listener rules.",
      i: "The authenticate-oidc listener action makes the ALB sign users in with the IdP before forwarding, with no application changes.",
    },
    referenceUrl: "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/elasticloadbalancing/latest/application/listener-authenticate-users.html",
