    id: "sec205",
    domain: "security-and-compliance",
    text: "A new administrator is reviewing how permissions are attached to IAM identities and finds both inline policies and managed policies in use. Which TWO statements correctly describe the differences between them? (Select TWO.)",
    options: [
      { id: "a", text: "An inline policy is embedded in a single user, group, or role and is deleted when that identity is deleted" },
      { id: "b", text: "Inline policies can be attached to multiple identities at once, while managed policies cannot" },
      { id: "c", text: "Managed policies can only be created by AWS; customers cannot create their own" },
      { id: "d", text: "Inline policies support versioning and rollback, while managed policies do not" },
      { id: "e", text: "Managed policies are evaluated before inline policies, so they always take precedence" },
      { id: "f", text: "A customer managed policy is a standalone object that can be attached to many identities and supports up to five stored versions for rollback" },
      { id: "g", text: "Inline policies are required for Service Control Policies to take effect" },
      { id: "h", text: "Managed policies can be attached to resources such as S3 buckets, while inline policies cannot" },
      { id: "i", text: "AWS managed policies are created and maintained by AWS and cannot be edited by customers" },
      { id: "j", text: "A managed policy has its own ARN, while an inline policy has no ARN of its own" },
    ],
    correctOptionIds: ["a", "f", "i", "j"],
    answerType: "multi",
    explanation: "Managed policies are standalone identity-based policies with their own ARNs that can be attached to many users, groups, and roles: AWS managed policies are maintained by AWS and cannot be edited, while customer managed policies are yours to edit and keep up to five versions for rollback. Inline policies are embedded in a single identity, have no ARN of their own, and are deleted along with that identity.",
    optionRationale: {
      a: "Correct. Inline policies live inside one identity and disappear with it.",
      b: "The opposite is true: managed policies are reusable, inline policies are not.",
      c: "Customers can create customer managed policies in addition to using AWS managed ones.",
      d: "Versioning applies to customer managed policies, not inline policies.",
      e: "All applicable policies are evaluated together; an explicit deny in any of them wins and there is no precedence by policy type.",
      f: "Correct. Customer managed policies are reusable objects with version history.",
      g: "SCPs are an AWS Organizations feature unrelated to inline policies.",
      h: "Resource-based policies such as bucket policies are a separate type; managed policies attach only to identities.",
      i: "AWS owns and updates AWS managed policies; customers can attach them but must create a customer managed policy to change them.",
      j: "Managed policies are standalone resources with ARNs, whereas inline policies exist only inside the identity that holds them.",
    },
    referenceUrl: "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/access_policies_managed-vs-inline.html",
    referenceLabel: "Managed policies and inline policies - IAM",
    consoleUrl: "https://us-east-1.console.aws.amazon.com/iam/home#/policies",
    consoleLabel: "IAM > Policies",
    diagram: "flowchart LR\n  MP[Customer managed policy\nversions v1..v5] --> U1[User A]\n  MP --> R1[Role B]\n  MP --> G1[Group C]\n  U2[User D] --- IP[Inline policy\n1:1, deleted with user]",
