    id: "sec225",
    domain: "security-and-compliance",
    text: "A central shared-services account hosts an S3 bucket of approved AMIs and scripts that every account in the company's AWS Organization must be able to read. New accounts are created every week, and the security team refuses to edit the bucket policy each time to add another account ID. Which approach meets the requirement with the LEAST ongoing effort while keeping the bucket private to the organization?",
    options: [
      { id: "a", text: "Set the bucket policy Principal to * and rely on S3 Block Public Access to keep it private" },
      { id: "b", text: "Write a bucket policy that allows s3:GetObject to any principal with a Condition on aws:PrincipalOrgID matching the organization's ID" },
      { id: "c", text: "Create an IAM user in the shared-services account and distribute its access keys to all accounts" },
      { id: "d", text: "Make the bucket public and enable AWS WAF in front of it" },
      { id: "e", text: "Use an SCP in the management account to grant every member account read access to the bucket" },
      { id: "f", text: "Enable S3 Transfer Acceleration so accounts can pull objects without permissions" },
      { id: "g", text: "Copy the bucket into every new account with S3 Batch Replication" },
      { id: "h", text: "Attach the bucket to a Transit Gateway shared through RAM" },
      { id: "i", text: "Write a bucket policy that allows s3:GetObject with a Condition on aws:PrincipalOrgPaths covering the organization root" },
    ],
    correctOptionIds: ["b", "i"],
    answerType: "single",
    explanation: "The global condition keys aws:PrincipalOrgID and aws:PrincipalOrgPaths identify the AWS Organization, and the path of OUs, that the calling principal's account belongs to. A bucket policy can allow access to any principal and restrict it with a condition matching the organization ID or the organization root path, so every current and future member account is covered automatically while principals outside the organization are denied; principals still need identity-based permissions for the S3 action.",
    optionRationale: {
      a: "Block Public Access would reject a policy with Principal * and no restricting condition, and would not scope access to the organization.",
      b: "Correct. aws:PrincipalOrgID grants organization-wide access without listing account IDs.",
      c: "Sharing long-term keys across accounts is insecure and against best practice.",
      d: "A public bucket exposes the data to everyone; WAF cannot be attached to S3.",
      e: "SCPs only restrict permissions; they never grant access.",
      f: "Transfer Acceleration affects speed, not authorization.",
      g: "Replicating to every account is high effort and duplicates data.",
      h: "Transit Gateway handles network routing, not S3 authorization.",
      i: "Every member account sits under the organization root, so a PrincipalOrgPaths condition on the root path covers new accounts automatically.",
    },
    referenceUrl: "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/reference_policies_condition-keys.html#condition-keys-principalorgid",
    referenceLabel: "AWS global condition context keys: aws:PrincipalOrgID",
