    correctOptionIds: ["a", "i"],
    answerType: "single",
    explanation: "AWS Config evaluates every bucket against a rule such as s3-bucket-versioning-enabled and flags NON_COMPLIANT ones. The fix then runs automatically, either as the rule's remediation action using a Systems Manager Automation runbook like AWS-ConfigureS3BucketVersioning, or through an EventBridge rule on the compliance change event that invokes a Lambda function to re-enable versioning.",
    optionRationale: {
      a: "Correct. Config rules detect drift and automatic remediation via SSM Automation fixes it.",
      b: "Lifecycle rules manage storage tiers, not versioning compliance.",
      c: "Macie discovers sensitive data; it does not enforce bucket settings.",
      d: "Denying bucket creation blocks work rather than fixing existing buckets.",
      e: "A size alarm has nothing to do with versioning.",
      f: "Trusted Advisor notifies but does not remediate automatically.",
      g: "AWS Backup protects data but does not toggle bucket versioning.",
      h: "Artifact provides AWS's audit reports, not remediation.",
      i: "Correct. Config flags buckets without versioning, and an EventBridge rule on the compliance change event invokes a Lambda function that re-enables versioning.",
    },
    referenceUrl: "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/config/latest/developerguide/remediation.html",
    referenceLabel: "Remediating noncompliant resources with AWS Config",
